Step back and marvel at the seventy-year arc we have traveled across Parts 1 and 2 of our story. In the 1950s, Cold War engineers needed to send radar coordinates down copper telephone wires built only for human voices, giving birth to the **modem**. Over the next half-century, every time humanity appeared to hit an unbreakable physical wall on that narrow $$300\text{--}3400\,\text{Hz}$$ phone line, mathematical ingenuity found another hidden dimension:

- From two-tone **FSK** whistles at $$300\,\text{baud}$$ to **PSK** timing shifts ($$1200\,\text{bps}$$) and **QAM constellation diagrams** ($$2400\,\text{bps}$$ V.22bis);
- From **echo cancellation**, **adaptive equalization**, and **Trellis coding** ($$9600$$ and $$14,400\,\text{bps}$$) to **V.34 multi-tone line probing** ($$33.6\,\text{kbps}$$);
- From Brent Townshend's **56k PCM quantization trick** (V.90/V.92) to bypassing the Central Office $$4\,\text{kHz}$$ voice filter altogether with **ADSL Discrete Multitone** and **DOCSIS cable QAM**.

The dial-up screech may have faded into fond nostalgia, alongside AOL CD-ROMs and late-night BBS door games—yet the constellation diagrams, equalizers, and multitone modulators forged on humble copper phone lines remain the beating mathematical heart of our connected world.
